# {{ title }}

If you've been using Obsidian, Notion, or any other tool that exports Markdown
or HTML, you can convert your existing notes to YAMD in one step.

::toc{depth=2}

---

## How to import

1. Open the command palette (:kbd[Cmd+Shift+P]).
2. Run **YAMD: Import Notes from Folder…**.
3. Choose the source format: **Obsidian vault**, **Notion Markdown export**, or
   **HTML files**.
4. Pick the folder on disk that contains your notes.
5. Enter a destination sub-folder name (or leave blank to import into the
   workspace root).
6. YAMD converts every file and reports how many notes were imported.

---

## What gets converted

### From Obsidian

| Obsidian syntax | YAMD equivalent |
| --------------- | --------------- |
| `[[WikiLink]]` | `[[WikiLink]]` (identical) |
| `![[embed.md]]` | `![[embed.yamd]]` |
| `![[image.png]]` | `![image](image.png)` |
| `> [!note]` callout | `:::note` container |
| `> [!tip]` callout | `:::tip` container |
| `> [!warning]` callout | `:::warning` container |
| `> [!danger]` / `[!error]` | `:::danger` container |
| `#hashtag` inline | `#hashtag` inline (identical) |
| `%%comment%%` | `%% comment %%` (identical) |
| YAML front matter | YAML front matter (preserved) |

Everything else (standard Markdown syntax, code fences, tables, task lists)
carries over unchanged because YAMD is a strict superset of GFM.

### From Notion

Notion exports folders of Markdown files, one per page. YAMD converts:

| Notion export | YAMD equivalent |
| ------------- | --------------- |
| `> 💡 tip` callouts | `:::tip` |
| `> ⚠️ warning` | `:::warning` |
| `> ❗ danger` | `:::danger` |
| `[Link text](Page%20Name.md)` | `[Link text](Page Name.md)` (URL-decoded) |
| Front matter injected | `---\ntitle: …\n---` |

The Notion page title (from the file name or the first `# heading`) becomes the
`title:` front matter key.

### From HTML

YAMD converts:

| HTML | YAMD |
| ---- | ---- |
| `<h1>`–`<h6>` | `#`–`######` headings |
| `<p>` | Paragraphs |
| `<strong>` / `<b>` | `**bold**` |
| `<em>` / `<i>` | `*italic*` |
| `<code>` | `` `code` `` |
| `<pre><code>` | Fenced code block |
| `<ul>` / `<ol>` | Bullet / numbered list |
| `<li>` with checkbox | Task list item |
| `<a href>` | `[text](url)` |
| `<img>` | `![alt](src)` |
| `<table>` | Pipe table |
| `<blockquote>` | `> blockquote` |

The page `<title>` or first `<h1>` becomes the `title:` front matter key.

---

## CSV to database

CSV files are not converted to notes — they become `.ydb` database files
instead. Column types are inferred from the first ten data rows:

| Values | Inferred type |
| ------ | ------------- |
| numbers | `number` |
| `true`/`false`, `yes`/`no` | `checkbox` |
| `YYYY-MM-DD` dates | `date` |
| a short fixed set of strings | `select` |
| anything else | `text` |

The first column becomes the `title` property. The `.ydb` file is ready to open
in the database view.

---

## After importing

- Run **YAMD: Open Study Dashboard** to see all the courses and deadlines that
  were in the imported notes' front matter.
- Check for `Y601` diagnostics in the **Problems** panel — these flag directives
  that didn't convert cleanly and need a manual look.
- Wikilinks between notes work automatically because YAMD resolves them by name.
  You don't need to update links even if files moved into sub-folders.

---

## What doesn't convert

- Obsidian plugins (Dataview, Excalidraw, graph view settings) — no equivalent
  yet; the raw plugin syntax is left as a comment.
- Notion sub-pages exported as separate files are imported as separate notes —
  parent/child relationships are not reconstructed.
- Images and attachments are **not moved** — they stay where they are, and the
  converted note references them with the original relative path.
